  <div class="prose-hgg mt-10">
    <h2>Use the installation hub as a project-scope checklist</h2>
    <p>A standby-generator installation is a coordinated project, not simply an appliance delivery. The equipment choice has to connect to a documented load plan, transfer equipment, a fuel source with adequate capacity, a compliant location, site preparation, permits or utility coordination where required, startup, and owner handoff.</p>
    <p>Start with <a href="/installation/what-to-expect/">what to expect during installation</a> to understand the sequence and who should own each task. Then use the <a href="/installation/generator-pad-and-placement/">pad and placement guide</a> to prepare questions about drainage, openings, exhaust, service access, snow/landscaping, and the exact manufacturer clearances. Requirements vary by model and jurisdiction; a general web page cannot approve a location.</p>
    <h2>Questions a complete proposal should answer</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Which exact generator and transfer-equipment models are being installed?</li>
      <li>Who verifies the electrical service, backed-up loads, motor starting, and any load management?</li>
      <li>Who verifies natural-gas pressure/flow or propane storage and regulator capacity?</li>
      <li>Who handles permits, inspections, utility coordination, trenching, restoration, and commissioning?</li>
      <li>What is excluded, an allowance, or dependent on site conditions discovered later?</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Use the <a href="/cost/standby-generator-quote-checklist/">16-line quote checklist</a> to put those answers side by side before comparing total price.</p>
    <h2>Safety first</h2>
    <p>We do not provide DIY electrical, gas, fuel, or transfer-equipment instructions. Read our <a href="/disclaimer/">safety disclaimer</a>, use the exact manufacturer installation manual, and rely on qualified local professionals and the authority having jurisdiction for the property.</p>
